    Objects created from reflection classes provided by this module are wrappers over the actual C++ objects;
    for this reason comparing different objects with the @ref logical_equality_operator "==" or
    @ref logical_inequality_operator "!=" operators will not return the expected results.  To perform
    comparisons on reflection objects, use the \c isEqual() methods such as
    @ref Qore::Reflection::AbstractClass::isEqual() "AbstractClass::isEqual()",
    @ref Qore::Reflection::AbstractMember::isEqual() "AbstractMember::isEqual()", or
    @ref Qore::Reflection::Namespace::isEqual() "Namespace::isEqual()", among others.

    @section reflection_caller_privilege Reflection and the Privileges of the Calling Code

    Reflection follows the privileges of the code that uses it. Cross-Program access depends on the operation:

    |!Operation|!Access rule
    |Structural inspection: namespace navigation, declarations, names, types, signatures, class hierarchy and fixed enum values|The caller's restricting domains and sandbox manager apply; missing injection or debugger capability does not prevent inspection
    |Reading constants, globals, object/static members or parameter defaults; writing values|Full Program access, including explicitly-allowed domains
    |Calling functions or methods, constructing, copying or destroying objects|Program access plus the caller's and target's functional-domain checks; public module classes also permit the exact imported-class rule below; object calls also check the object's Program
    |Reading member defaults or constructing typed hashes with <tt>doCast*</tt>|Full Program access, retaining a narrow exception for trusted user-module code that lacks the target's injection or debugger capability

    Full Program access is allowed from the target @ref Qore::Program "Program", from a Program that created it
    directly or transitively (including independent Programs), or from code at least as privileged as it. The latter
    must restrict no functional domain the target allows, allow every explicitly-allowed domain the target allows,
    and have no governing sandbox manager other than the target's own. Otherwise \c REFLECTION-ACCESS-ERROR is raised.
    A module reflecting into another user module also has full access when the application %Program its module call
    chain runs for has it: a module %Program inherits the options of the %Program that loaded it first, so a library
    module may lack an explicitly-allowed domain that a module loaded later has, while its application has it.
    Structural navigation uses the same creator, restricting-domain and sandbox rules, but does not compare
    explicitly-allowed domains. Existing metadata-only accessors on a supplied handle retain their behavior.

    A user module %Program has no sandbox manager of its own: its code runs under the
    @ref Qore::SandboxManager "SandboxManager" of the code that calls it, so a caller governed by a sandbox manager may
    reach a user module %Program as when it calls the module directly, and the module's filesystem and network access
    remains subject to the caller's sandbox. Any other %Program without a sandbox manager cannot be reached by a
    caller governed by one.

    Modules may reflect back into a caller already on their active module call chain, up to the first application
    Program. For class calls, a module may also use an exact public user-module class imported by that application,
    even when the defining module is inactive and enables injection or debugging. This preserves cross-module
    data-provider factories. The class ID and defining Program must match the import; builtin classes, private
    module classes and handles bound to unrelated Programs do not qualify. Restricting domains, the sandbox-manager
    rule and callable-domain checks still apply; the constructed class runs under the application's sandbox. This
    grants no access to the module's globals or other symbols.

    Inspection never authorizes execution or state access: a caller can discover a function or typed hash without
    being allowed to call the function or evaluate the hash's initializers. Builtin functions and constructors also
    require full access: they can create Programs or invoke target constructors, even without a user-code body. Constants remain value access because they may contain
    objects or call references and may resolve deferred AOT references; enum values are fixed scalar declarations.

    Member defaults can evaluate arbitrary expressions. The trusted user-module exception applies only to
    @ref Qore::Reflection::AbstractMember::getDefaultValue() "AbstractMember::getDefaultValue()" and the
    <tt>TypedHash::doCast*</tt> methods that fill missing members, allowing
    @ref dataproviderintro "DataProvider" to consume types supplied by Programs using
    @ref allow-injection "%allow-injection" or @ref allow-debugger "%allow-debugger" from source or AOT modules.
    Restricting domains and sandbox-manager checks still apply. Other callers need full access to evaluate defaults.
    @ref Qore::Reflection::Type::getDefaultValue() "Type::getDefaultValue()" returns intrinsic empty/zero type
    defaults and does not evaluate member initializers.

    A class, typed hash or enum inherited or imported from another Program provides no namespace path into its
    declaring Program: reflection returns the namespace with the same path in the reflection object's own Program.

    @subsection reflection_member_access Member Access of Sandboxed and Restricted Callers

    Code that a @ref Qore::SandboxManager "SandboxManager" governs, also through a policy barrier
    (@ref Qore::SandboxManager::callWithSystemPolicy() "SandboxManager::callWithSystemPolicy()") or in a system-policy
    capability (@ref Qore::SandboxManager::createSystemPolicyCall() "SandboxManager::createSystemPolicyCall()"), and
    code whose parse options restrict a functional domain (for example
    @ref Qore::PO_NO_FILESYSTEM "PO_NO_FILESYSTEM"), uses the elements of a class through reflection as it could use
    them in source code: a \c private element only from the class that declares it and the classes in its hierarchy,
    and a @ref private_members "private:internal" element only from the class that declares it, the class context
    being the one of the code that calls the reflection API.  The rule applies when reading or writing a member or a
    static member, reading a constant or a member's default value, and calling a method, a method variant, a
    constructor, a copy method, a destructor or a pseudo-method; a call that selects a method's variant for its
    arguments requires every variant of the method to be accessible.  Otherwise \c REFLECTION-ACCESS-ERROR is
    raised.  Inspecting declarations, such as listing members and methods with their access, is not affected.

    Other code may use any element of a class through reflection, whatever its access, as before: debuggers and
    serializers rely on it.  Trusted code must therefore not rely on @ref private_members "private:internal" alone
    to keep a capability or other state from code that is itself trusted.

    @subsection reflection_module_exports The Export Boundary of User Modules

    The same callers (code that a @ref Qore::SandboxManager "SandboxManager" governs, or whose parse options restrict
    a functional domain) reach through reflection only what a @ref user_modules "user module" exports, that is, what
    it declares \c public, and builtin declarations, also when they reflect on the module's own %Program (for example
    through <tt>Class::getClass(obj).getNamespace()</tt> for an object that module code created):
    - a lookup by name in a namespace of the module, such as
      @ref Qore::Reflection::Namespace::getFunction() "Namespace::getFunction()", or by path or pattern in the
      module's %Program, does not find a declaration that the module does not export, nor anything in a namespace that
      it does not export, and raises the same exception as for a declaration that does not exist; a listing, such as
      @ref Qore::Reflection::Namespace::getFunctions() "Namespace::getFunctions()", leaves it out
    - a reflection handle of such a function, global variable, constant or typed hash, which the module itself created
      and handed out, cannot call the function, read or write the variable, read the constant or evaluate the typed
      hash's initializers
    - a class that the module does not export cannot be constructed, and its static methods, static members,
      constants and member initializers cannot be used; its normal methods and members can be used with an object of
      the class, subject to @ref reflection_member_access "member access", as in source code

    Otherwise \c REFLECTION-ACCESS-ERROR is raised.  A declaration that the module imported from another module is
    checked against the module that declares it: what that module exports remains reachable.  Code that runs in the
    module's own %Program, and other callers, reach every declaration, as before.

